# Every text file is LF in the index and LF on checkout, on every platform.
#
# This is not a style preference. The generated registry records a sha256 for
# each of the 112 files it distributes, computed from the file as it sits in
# the working tree. With `core.autocrlf` enabled — as it is on at least one
# maintainer machine — and no attributes file, 51 of those 112 files were
# checked out CRLF on Windows and LF everywhere else, so the recorded hashes
# were correct on exactly one operating system.
#
# Three things that breaks, all of them quietly:
#
#   * `dart run tool/registry_builder/bin/build.dart .` produces different
#     hashes per platform, so the reproducibility gate (`git diff --exit-code`
#     after a rebuild) fails on whichever platform did not write the registry.
#   * A consumer verifying a downloaded payload against its declared sha256
#     fails for reasons that have nothing to do with the payload.
#   * `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` records a hash per redistributed file. A hash
#     that only holds on one platform is not a provenance record.
#
# `text=auto` still detects binary content and leaves it alone; the explicit
# rules below exist so that detection is never the thing standing between a
# font and corruption.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Redistributed binaries and build inputs. Never translate these.
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.ico   binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.mp4   binary
*.zip   binary
*.jar   binary
*.keystore binary

# Windows-only tooling that other tools expect to find with CRLF.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
